From 2c4fe8d7bbd87b14c072b1b17cec25f197c48592 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:17:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] VRR video only where the display has no mode at
 the video's rate, and its signal measured

On an RTX 4080 SUPER with an LG C5 and a Samsung Odyssey G60SD, with the
settings of VRR on, the LG C5 said 120 Hz for videos of 23.976 that Kodi played at 47.952 Hz with VRR
(NV_VRR_ACTIVE 1), in 3840x2160 at 120 Hz and in 2560x1440 at 119.998 Hz, a resolution of the menus that
the Samsung had left; the G60SD said 359.999 Hz.

What the signal was: Kodi's own measure of when each picture reached the output (CPresentLag, by the
out-fence of each flip) put those pictures within 1.3 to 1.6 ms of the player's clock over 410 to 1392
pictures, where a fixed refresh at 120 Hz would have spread them over its 8.3 ms: the source sent each
picture at its time. Whether a display applies the VRR that a source sends cannot be known: HDMI has
no register for it (the SCDC of HDMI 2.1 has none), and the EDID of the LG C5 and of the G60SD was the
same in every test, whatever their own VRR settings were. A display may show the refresh rate of the
mode in its own information. So:

- A video goes at its own rate with VRR only where the display leaves no other way
  (VRR_VIDEO::Automatic()): after Kodi's whole choice of a mode (its whitelist, the user's or its
  default), where that stays in the mode of the menus, whose refresh is no whole multiple of the frame
  rate, and the display has no mode at the frame rate or twice it at any resolution
  (DisplayHasModeFor(), all its progressive 2D modes). A display with such a mode (the LG C5 at
  3840x2160 and 23.976 Hz) always gets its change of mode, which shows the video exactly, also where
  Kodi's default whitelist does not reach that mode from the resolution of the menus (2560x1440 on the
  LG C5: the video plays as Kodi chose, and kodi.log says so). Nothing is taken before
  Kodi's choice any more.
- "Video at its own rate with VRR (no change of mode)" becomes "Video with VRR where the display has
  no mode at its rate": it switches the above, on by default; off, videos play as before. Its help
  and that of "HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)" say so, in English and the three Spanish languages.
  The modes of the menus keep HDMI VRR (CHdmiVrrOff::Allowed()) only on a display where it applies:
  the setting on, VRR declared, no mode of the rate of films or twice it at any resolution
  (AutoForDisplay()).
- Where nvidia-drm says that VRR is active (NV_VRR_ACTIVE), the signal is measured too
  (CStartCheck::FenceInterval()): the out-fence of each paced flip, read with the next one, against the
  time asked for it. With a fixed refresh a flip completes at the next refresh of the mode (at 47.952
  Hz in a mode of 120 Hz every interval is 4.2 ms off): where more than a quarter of the intervals are
  off by over 1 ms, OUTPUT_FIXED, and the video plays as Kodi chose; none read in 5 s, UNMEASURED.
  Where a fixed refresh would itself be off in under half of the intervals (the mode's rate nearly a
  whole multiple of the video's: 71.928 Hz in 359.999 Hz, one refresh in 196 longer), the fences
  cannot tell, and the driver's word holds; the log says which. kodi.log has the out-fences against
  the times asked every 30 s and at the end of the video.
- The VRR row of the player information says "On at the output: the refresh follows the video".

Checked: the host tests (vrr_video_test.cpp: the modes of the G60SD and of the LG C5, the LG C5 with
the menus at 2560x1440, the fences of a fixed refresh and of VRR; vrr_gui_test.cpp: Allowed()), with
their mutants; the changed files and the sources that include them compile with the commands of the
image's build. Not run on a GPU.
